From: François Guerraz <kubrick@fgv6.net>
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: qualcomm: ppe: rtl8_4s awareness and the S-R
 topology resolver

With rtl8_4s bound, the RTL8372N can label the frames it hands the CPU
with an 802.1ad S-tag naming the ingress port (3900 + p), in front of
the client's own C-tag, if any. That lets the PPE in front of the
conduit tell uploads apart by switch port and VLAN, and address a
download to a port and VLAN with an S-tag (3910 + p) and a C-tag: the
S-R mode, which later patches add. This one adds what both directions
share, the topology resolver, with no caller yet, and lets profile E
run under rtl8_4s.

Profile E: rtl8_4s transmits with rtl8_4's 8 byte head tag, armed
switch or not, so the head tag emulation of the download nexthops is
the same. ppe_offload_dsa_egress() and the conduit lookup at enable
accept either tagger. The S-R mode will own the egress S-TPID while it
is not off, and E is then to stay off; until it exists, E runs as with
rtl8_4. Uploads from either tagger are still declined.

The resolver (ppe_sr_topo.c) is pure: no kernel call, so that it is
tested in user space on fake bridges. For a usable port p of the S-R
bridge (in the switch's S-R port mask, bridged by the switch in that
bridge with its VLAN filtering, in no LAG, with no VLAN upper) it makes:

- U(p) = (p, untagged, u) iff p egresses untagged in exactly one VLAN
  u, its PVID is u, the bridge itself is a member of u (BRENTRY), and u
  has an L3 device whose MAC address is the conduit's;
- T(p, v) = (p, tagged, v) iff p is an egress-tagged member of v, the
  bridge itself is a member of v, and v has such an L3 device.

VID 0, 4095 and the rtl8_4s reserved VIDs 3900-3919 never make a class.
An upload without a VLAN key, or a download without a VLAN push, is
U(p)'s; one keyed on, or pushing, VLAN v is T(p, v)'s. Otherwise the
resolver says why: an unusable port, an invalid or reserved VID, no L3
device, not a member, the other form, no or two untagged VLANs, a PVID
mismatch, the bridge not a member, or an L3 device with another MAC.
A download also gets its MTU cap: the smallest of the port's, the
bridge's, the L3 device's and the conduit's MTU less the 8 bytes of
the two tags. The classes in a scope, at most 48, are the identities
to program.

The L3 devices are the bridge's running 802.1Q uppers and the bridge
itself, if running, for its PVID; devices of one VID are merged, and
only the 32 lowest VIDs are kept (the rest decline as having none).
The S-R bridge is the one bridge the ports of the mask are in; with
two, no port is usable.

ppe_sr.c reads all of that under RTNL (ppe_sr_topo_collect()) and
answers the resolver's membership questions from br_vlan_get_info()
(ppe_sr_topo_vinfo()). The bridge has no exported walk of a port's
VLANs, so every VID of every usable port is asked, once per resolve.

Signed-off-by: François Guerraz <kubrick@fgv6.net>
